## Proof

Let $S = a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{2n+1}$. For each index $i$, removing $a_i$ leaves $2n$ integers that can be split into two sets of size $n$ with equal sums. Denote this common sum by
$$f(i) = \frac{S - a_i}{2}.$$

**Claim:** For any two distinct indices $i, j$, we have $a_i = a_j$.

Fix distinct $i, j$. When $a_i$ is removed, let the partition be $B_i, C_i$ with $|B_i|=|C_i|=n$ and $\sum B_i = \sum C_i = f(i)$. When $a_j$ is removed, let the partition be $B_j, C_j$ with $|B_j|=|C_j|=n$ and $\sum B_j = \sum C_j = f(j)$.

Note $B_i, C_i$ partition $\{a_1,\dots,a_{2n+1}\}\setminus\{a_i\}$ and $B_j, C_j$ partition $\{a_1,\dots,a_{2n+1}\}\setminus\{a_j\}$. Define four sets:
- $X = B_i \cap B_j$, $\quad Y = B_i \cap C_j$, $\quad Z = C_i \cap B_j$, $\quad W = C_i \cap C_j$.

These partition the $2n-1$ elements other than $a_i$ and $a_j$.

**Sizes.** Since $a_j \notin B_j$ and $a_i \notin B_i$:
$$|X|+|Y| = |B_i| = n, \quad |Z|+|W| = |C_i| = n, \quad |X|+|Z| = |B_j| = n, \quad |Y|+|W| = |C_j| = n.$$
From $|X|+|Y| = |X|+|Z|$: $|Y| = |Z|$. From $|X|+|Z| = |Y|+|W|$ and $|Y|=|Z|$: $|X| = |W|$.

**Sums.**
$$\textstyle\sum X + \sum Y = f(i), \quad \sum Z + \sum W = f(i), \quad \sum X + \sum Z = f(j), \quad \sum Y + \sum W = f(j).$$

From the first two: $\sum X - \sum W = \sum Z - \sum Y$.  
From the last two: $\sum X - \sum W = \sum Y - \sum Z$.

Equating the right-hand sides: $\sum Z - \sum Y = \sum Y - \sum Z$, so $2(\sum Z - \sum Y) = 0$, giving
$$\textstyle\sum Y = \sum Z.$$

Now from $\sum X + \sum Y = f(i)$ and $\sum X + \sum Z = f(j)$, using $\sum Y = \sum Z$:
$$f(i) = f(j).$$

Substituting the definition of $f$:
$$\frac{S - a_i}{2} = \frac{S - a_j}{2} \implies a_i = a_j.$$

Since $i$ and $j$ were arbitrary, we conclude
$$a_1 = a_2 = \cdots = a_{2n+1}. \qquad \blacksquare$$

